implementationPlan.description:
Add readRepositorySource(projectRoot, commit, queries, options?: { limits?, run?, head?, slots? }): Promise<RepositorySource>. It is read-only, must be called outside the state mutex, and is bounded by ONE shared deadline: const git = await openProjectGit(projectRoot, limitedRun(slots, run), limits.timeBudgetMs, limitedHead(slots, head)). This step covers the trust envelope, the manifest, path classification and reads; step 5 adds searches and the total byte cap.
1. validateSourceRequest runs first: INVALID_INPUT, nothing spawned.
2. Any openProjectGit failure (Git missing, timeout, root ending in /*, Git toplevel is not the project root (a project nested in another repository), partial-clone/promisor/core.worktree) makes every item 'unavailable' with reason `repository: <message first line>`, plus the limitation 'repository-unavailable'. Never fall back to reading the filesystem.
3. git('rev-parse --verify', ['rev-parse', '--verify', '--end-of-options', `${commit}^{commit}`]) must print exactly the commit. A tree or blob id, or an unknown object, makes every item 'unavailable' with reason `manifest: <message first line>`.
4. ONE listing: git('ls-tree', ['--literal-pathspecs', 'ls-tree', '-z', '-l', '--full-tree', commit, '--', ...pathspecs], { maxBuffer: 1048576 }), where pathspecs are every read path and search scope plus all their ancestor prefixes, deduplicated. Parse each NUL-terminated record with /^(100644|100755|120000|040000|160000) (blob|tree|commit) ([0-9a-f]{40}|[0-9a-f]{64}) +(\d+|-)\t([\s\S]+)$/. Unterminated or unparsable output makes the affected items 'unavailable' with 'git ls-tree: unparsable output'. Paths of unrelated children are only used for prefix comparison and need no normalization.
Git behavior verified on 2.43, which the classifier must handle: when one pathspec forces descent into a directory D, ls-tree prints D's direct children instead of D itself. So D exists as a tree iff it is printed with 040000 OR any printed entry starts with `D/`. A blocking symlink (120000) or gitlink (160000) is printed only because its own prefix is a pathspec, which is why ancestors are always listed.
Classify each read path and scope, in order:
- an ancestor that is 120000: refused 'symlink-ancestor';
- an ancestor that is 160000: refused 'submodule';
- an ancestor that is a regular blob: missing 'not-in-manifest';
- the entry itself 120000: refused 'symlink';
- the entry itself 160000: refused 'submodule';
- a tree: refused 'not-a-file' for a read, a valid scope for a search;
- absent: missing 'not-in-manifest' for a read, missing 'scope-not-in-manifest' for a scope.
Git paths are exact and case-sensitive on every platform. Symlinks are never followed.
5. Reads of regular blobs (100644/100755) use git.head('cat-file', ['cat-file', 'blob', oid], limits.maxFileBytes):
- exitCode not 0: 'unavailable' `git cat-file: <stderr>`;
- not truncated but the byte length differs from the ls-tree size: 'unavailable' 'size-mismatch';
- any NUL byte: refused 'binary';
- when truncated, cut back to the last complete UTF-8 sequence first;
- decode with new TextDecoder('utf-8', { fatal: true }); a failure is refused 'not-utf8';
- size above maxFileBytes: truncated 'file-byte-cap' with the decoded prefix;
- otherwise served.
Always report path, oid, mode, size and bytes.
6. A per-item failure (budget exhausted, slot timeout, killed, ENOENT) makes only that item 'unavailable' with the error's first line. Items run concurrently through the slots. items.length always equals queries.length, complete is true iff every item is served, and the limitation 'committed-bytes-only' is always present.
Tests (real Git, repositorySource.test.ts). The fixture helper builds disposable repositories under os.tmpdir() only, with plumbing as in src/tools/submitPlan.test.ts cachedBranch(): `hash-object -w --no-filters --stdin`, `update-index --add --cacheinfo <mode>,<oid>,<path>` (120000/160000 entries need no filesystem symlink, so this runs on Windows), write-tree, commit-tree, update-ref refs/heads/fixture. It runs with an env that strips EVERY inherited GIT_* variable, sets GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM=1 and GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL to the null device, sets fixture author/committer, and uses its own GIT_INDEX_FILE inside the fixture .git. It must never write to the project checkout. Pass timeBudgetMs 10000 for slow Windows runners.
Cases, each asserting exact status, reason and content:
(1) a served read gives exact bytes, oid, mode 100644, size and bytes, complete true; mode 100755 is also served;
(2) a missing path gives missing/not-in-manifest while a sibling query is still served, complete false, items in query order;
(3) a symlink entry gives refused/symlink with no content;
(4) lnk/x.ts under a symlinked directory gives refused/symlink-ancestor;
(5) a gitlink at the path and as an ancestor gives refused/submodule;
(6) a directory read gives refused/not-a-file, also when another query reads a file inside it (the descent case);
(7) a path whose ancestor is a regular file gives missing/not-in-manifest;
(8) a blob with NUL gives refused/binary;
(9) a latin-1 blob gives refused/not-utf8;
(10) a file over a lowered maxFileBytes, with a multi-byte character straddling the cap, gives truncated/file-byte-cap; content is valid UTF-8, bytes <= cap, size is the full size;
(11) immutability: after a second commit changes the file AND the fixture working-tree file is rewritten, reading the first commit returns the first commit's bytes;
(12) a project directory nested inside the fixture repository makes every item unavailable, with a reason naming the toplevel mismatch;
(13) a well-formed unknown commit, and a tree id passed as the commit, make every item unavailable with a 'manifest:' reason;
(14) with fake run/head runners and a mocked clock, an exhausted deadline makes the remaining items unavailable /budget exhausted/ and no runner call starts after it;
(15) an invalid request through readRepositorySource gives INVALID_INPUT with zero runner calls.

Complete readRepositorySource with searches, the deterministic total byte cap and limitations.
Search runs git.head('grep', ['--literal-pathspecs', 'grep', '-F', '-z', '-n', '-I', '--no-color', '--full-name', '-e', literal, commit, '--', ...scopes], limits.maxSearchBytes), and only when every scope classified in step 4 as a valid tree or regular file. Otherwise the item takes that scope's missing/refused status and reason: a missing scope must never become an empty served search, which would be a false negative. Never pass --no-index, --untracked, --cached or --recurse-submodules. Tree grep reads committed blobs only and skips symlink and gitlink entries (verified on git 2.43).
Parse output records `<commit>:<path>\0<line>\0<text>\n` (verified). Require the exact `${commit}:` prefix, a decimal line >= 1, path and text that decode as fatal UTF-8, and a path that passes the step-2 path rule. Outcomes:
- exit 1: served with matches [] (a real negative for this commit, literal and scope set only);
- any other non-zero exit: 'unavailable' `git grep: <stderr>`;
- truncated output: parse complete records only, truncated 'search-byte-cap';
- more than maxMatches: keep the first maxMatches, truncated 'match-cap';
- any record dropped as undecodable or unrepresentable: truncated 'undecodable-match'.
Matches keep Git's output order.
Apply the total byte cap after every item settles, walking items in QUERY order, so the result is deterministic whatever the completion order. An item whose bytes would push the running total past maxTotalBytes becomes omitted 'total-byte-cap', with content/matches removed and bytes 0.
Limitations, sorted and unique:
- always 'committed-bytes-only' (uncommitted worktree/overlay bytes are never part of the manifest);
- 'literal-search-only' when any search ran (patterns and callers are textual matches, not resolved references; semantic analysis is C03's);
- 'repository-unavailable' as in step 4.
Tests (real Git unless noted):
(1) a served search returns exact path (one containing a space), line and text;
(2) zero matches gives served with [];
(3) a missing scope gives missing/scope-not-in-manifest, not served-empty; a symlinked scope gives refused/symlink; a scope under a gitlink gives refused/submodule;
(4) a file scope and a directory scope both work;
(5) a lowered maxSearchBytes gives truncated/search-byte-cap with only complete records; maxMatches 1 gives truncated/match-cap;
(6) a symlink whose target text contains the literal produces no match;
(7) items past maxTotalBytes in query order become omitted/total-byte-cap, and result bytes <= the cap;
(8) a fake head runner completing in reverse query order omits exactly the same items as natural order;
(9) limitations are exactly ['committed-bytes-only'] for reads only and ['committed-bytes-only', 'literal-search-only'] once a search ran.
Error handling: a grep failure affects only its own item.
